        "reasoning": "Both earlier answers agree with the captured worked solutions. For q-42264, matrix multiplication is associative, so II always holds. I and III fail, for example, with A=diag(1,0), B having its sole nonzero entry B_{12}=1, and C=0: AB=B while BA=0. For q-130455, associativity makes I always true. Distribution gives A(B-C)=AB-AC, so II and III are not guaranteed. For a counterexample in dimension 6, take A with A_{11}=1, C with C_{12}=1, all other entries zero, and B=0. Then AC=C, CA=0, and A(B-C)=-C differs from AB-CA=0.",
