        "problem": "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-1=4y \\\\ y=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-4y=1 \\\\ 0x+y=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
